{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "cb78f63e-cfe7-4242-b327-82d03a81bc69",
  "title": "std::autodiff: Differentiation in the Compiler",
  "created_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": "// ── Forward mode: dual numbers ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A dual number a + b·ε carries a value in `re` and a derivative in `eps`,\n// with the defining rule ε² = 0. Every arithmetic impl below propagates the\n// chain rule for free: evaluate f at Dual::var(x) and read f'(x) off `.eps`.\n\nuse std::ops::{Add, Div, Mul, Neg, Sub};\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub struct Dual {\n    /// The value f(x).\n    pub re: f64,\n    /// The derivative carried alongside it (the coefficient of ε).\n    pub eps: f64,\n}\n\nimpl Dual {\n    /// The independent variable: value x, seeded derivative 1 (dx/dx = 1).\n    pub fn var(x: f64) -> Self {\n        Dual { re: x, eps: 1.0 }\n    }\n\n    /// A constant: value x, derivative 0.\n    pub fn constant(x: f64) -> Self {\n        Dual { re: x, eps: 0.0 }\n    }\n\n    /// sin(a + bε) = sin a + (b·cos a) ε\n    pub fn sin(self) -> Self {\n        Dual { re: self.re.sin(), eps: self.eps * self.re.cos() }\n    }\n\n    /// cos(a + bε) = cos a − (b·sin a) ε\n    pub fn cos(self) -> Self {\n        Dual { re: self.re.cos(), eps: -self.eps * self.re.sin() }\n    }\n\n    /// exp(a + bε) = eᵃ + (b·eᵃ) ε\n    pub fn exp(self) -> Self {\n        let e = self.re.exp();\n        Dual { re: e, eps: self.eps * e }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Add for Dual {\n    type Output = Dual;\n    fn add(self, o: Dual) -> Dual {\n        Dual { re: self.re + o.re, eps: self.eps + o.eps }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Sub for Dual {\n    type Output = Dual;\n    fn sub(self, o: Dual) -> Dual {\n        Dual { re: self.re - o.re, eps: self.eps - o.eps }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Mul for Dual {\n    type Output = Dual;\n    // (a + bε)(c + dε) = ac + (ad + bc) ε   (the ε² term vanishes)\n    fn mul(self, o: Dual) -> Dual {\n        Dual { re: self.re * o.re, eps: self.re * o.eps + self.eps * o.re }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Div for Dual {\n    type Output = Dual;\n    // (a + bε)/(c + dε) = a/c + (bc − ad)/c² ε\n    fn div(self, o: Dual) -> Dual {\n        Dual {\n            re: self.re / o.re,\n            eps: (self.eps * o.re - self.re * o.eps) / (o.re * o.re),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Neg for Dual {\n    type Output = Dual;\n    fn neg(self) -> Dual {\n        Dual { re: -self.re, eps: -self.eps }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The wave packet f(x) = sin(x)·e^(−x²/2), written in ordinary Rust with the\n/// argument as a dual number. Evaluating it seeds and threads the derivative\n/// automatically, so `.eps` of the result is exactly f'(x).\npub fn wave_packet(x: Dual) -> Dual {\n    let half = Dual::constant(0.5);\n    x.sin() * (-(half * x * x)).exp()\n}\n\n// ── Reverse mode: a Wengert-list tape ────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Every operation appends a node recording its parents and the local partial\n// derivative ∂(this)/∂(parent). One forward sweep fills values; one backward\n// sweep seeds the output adjoint to 1 and accumulates adjoints down the list,\n// yielding the whole gradient in a single pass regardless of input count.\n\nuse std::cell::RefCell;\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Node {\n    /// Up to two parents, each paired with the local partial ∂(this)/∂(parent).\n    /// A leaf points at itself with weight 0 (a self-edge that contributes\n    /// nothing and is skipped during the backward sweep).\n    parents: [(usize, f64); 2],\n}\n\npub struct Tape {\n    nodes: RefCell<Vec<Node>>,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Tape {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Tape::new()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Tape {\n    pub fn new() -> Self {\n        Tape { nodes: RefCell::new(Vec::new()) }\n    }\n\n    /// Register an independent variable (a leaf of the graph).\n    pub fn var(&self, value: f64) -> Var<'_> {\n        let mut nodes = self.nodes.borrow_mut();\n        let index = nodes.len();\n        nodes.push(Node { parents: [(index, 0.0), (index, 0.0)] });\n        Var { tape: self, value, index }\n    }\n\n    fn push(&self, value: f64, parents: [(usize, f64); 2]) -> Var<'_> {\n        let mut nodes = self.nodes.borrow_mut();\n        let index = nodes.len();\n        nodes.push(Node { parents });\n        Var { tape: self, value, index }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\npub struct Var<'t> {\n    tape: &'t Tape,\n    pub value: f64,\n    index: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'t> Var<'t> {\n    pub fn sin(self) -> Var<'t> {\n        self.tape.push(\n            self.value.sin(),\n            [(self.index, self.value.cos()), (self.index, 0.0)],\n        )\n    }\n\n    pub fn exp(self) -> Var<'t> {\n        let e = self.value.exp();\n        self.tape.push(e, [(self.index, e), (self.index, 0.0)])\n    }\n\n    /// Run the backward sweep from this node and return the gradient vector,\n    /// indexed by node id. Read a leaf's partial with `grad[leaf.id()]`.\n    pub fn grad(self) -> Vec<f64> {\n        let nodes = self.tape.nodes.borrow();\n        let mut grad = vec![0.0; nodes.len()];\n        grad[self.index] = 1.0;\n        for i in (0..nodes.len()).rev() {\n            let adjoint = grad[i];\n            if adjoint == 0.0 {\n                continue;\n            }\n            for (parent, weight) in nodes[i].parents {\n                if parent != i {\n                    grad[parent] += adjoint * weight;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        grad\n    }\n\n    /// This variable's node id, for indexing into the gradient vector.\n    pub fn id(self) -> usize {\n        self.index\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'t> Add for Var<'t> {\n    type Output = Var<'t>;\n    fn add(self, o: Var<'t>) -> Var<'t> {\n        self.tape.push(self.value + o.value, [(self.index, 1.0), (o.index, 1.0)])\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'t> Sub for Var<'t> {\n    type Output = Var<'t>;\n    fn sub(self, o: Var<'t>) -> Var<'t> {\n        self.tape.push(self.value - o.value, [(self.index, 1.0), (o.index, -1.0)])\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'t> Mul for Var<'t> {\n    type Output = Var<'t>;\n    // ∂(xy)/∂x = y, ∂(xy)/∂y = x\n    fn mul(self, o: Var<'t>) -> Var<'t> {\n        self.tape.push(self.value * o.value, [(self.index, o.value), (o.index, self.value)])\n    }\n}\n",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_intro",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "The pitch",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Differentiation, done by the compiler\n\n**Derivatives quietly run modern computing.** Training a neural network is gradient descent on a loss function; a physics engine integrates forces, which are themselves derivatives of an energy; every \"how does the output move when I nudge the input\" question is a derivative wearing a trench coat. So it matters, a great deal, how fast and how *accurately* you can compute them.\n\nRust's answer is unusually ambitious: put automatic differentiation **inside the compiler**. The `std::autodiff` feature (nightly, `-Zautodiff`) hands your function to [Enzyme](https://enzyme.mit.edu/), an LLVM plugin that differentiates your code at the *LLVM-IR level, after optimization*. As of the [Rust Project Goals update from April 2026](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2026/05/18/project-goals-2026-04/), it is partially wired into CI and usable on nightly today if you supply the Enzyme library yourself. More on the real thing at the end.\n\nHere is the fun part: **the two algorithms the compiler automates are simple enough to build from scratch, and this notebook does exactly that.** We will hand-roll forward-mode AD with dual numbers, plot a function against its own derivative without writing a line of calculus ourselves, quantify *how* exact it is against finite differences, then build the reverse-mode tape that powers every deep-learning framework in use today. Then we will look at what `std::autodiff` actually does, and why doing it in the compiler is the whole point.\n\n*No `#[derive(Calculus)]`. Just algebra that carries a derivative along for the ride.*"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_act1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Forward mode: dual numbers",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 1: forward mode, or how to differentiate with algebra\n\nA **dual number** looks like $a + b\\,\\varepsilon$, where $\\varepsilon$ is a symbol with exactly one rule: $\\varepsilon^2 = 0$. It is the same move as the complex numbers ($i^2 = -1$), pointed at calculus instead of geometry.\n\nWatch what happens when you push a dual number through any analytic function and expand it as a Taylor series:\n\n$$f(a + b\\,\\varepsilon) = f(a) + b\\,f'(a)\\,\\varepsilon + \\frac{b^2 f''(a)}{2}\\,\\varepsilon^2 + \\cdots = f(a) + b\\,f'(a)\\,\\varepsilon$$\n\n**Every term past the first derivative dies**, because it carries an $\\varepsilon^2$ or higher. So if you evaluate $f$ at $a + 1\\cdot\\varepsilon$, the real part is $f(a)$ and the $\\varepsilon$ coefficient is *exactly* $f'(a)$. No limit, no small step, no symbolic manipulation: the derivative rides along in the $\\varepsilon$ slot, and the chain rule falls out of ordinary multiplication.\n\nThe whole engine is one struct and a handful of operator `impl`s (see the **`Dual`** type in this notebook's shared source). Each operation encodes a derivative rule you already know by heart: the product rule lives in `Mul`, the quotient rule in `Div`, and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x = \\cos x$ in `Dual::sin`. Our test function is a **Gaussian-damped sine**, a little wave packet:\n\n$$f(x) = \\sin(x)\\,e^{-x^2/2}$$"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_point",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "f and f' at a point",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Seed x = 1 with derivative 1, then evaluate the packet exactly once.\nlet x0 = 1.0_f64;\nlet d = shared::wave_packet(shared::Dual::var(x0));\n\n// Hand-derived derivative, for comparison:\n//   f'(x) = e^(−x²/2) · (cos x − x · sin x)\nlet analytic = (-0.5 * x0 * x0).exp() * (x0.cos() - x0 * x0.sin());\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"quantity\", \"value\"],\n    vec![\n        vec![\"x\".to_string(), format!(\"{x0}\")],\n        vec![\"f(x) = sin(x)·e^(−x²/2)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.12}\", d.re)],\n        vec![\"f'(x)  (dual number .eps)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.12}\", d.eps)],\n        vec![\"f'(x)  (hand-derived)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.12}\", analytic)],\n        vec![\"absolute difference\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.1e}\", (d.eps - analytic).abs())],\n    ],\n)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_plot",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "f vs f' across the packet",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\nuse crate::shared::Dual;\n\n// Sample f and its derivative across the wave packet. Each sample seeds a\n// dual number at x, then reads the value off `.re` and the derivative off\n// `.eps`; one evaluation yields both curves, no separate derivative code.\nlet n = 600usize;\nlet xs: Vec<f64> = (0..=n).map(|i| -6.0 + 12.0 * i as f64 / n as f64).collect();\nlet f_pts: Vec<(f64, f64)> = xs.iter().map(|&x| (x, shared::wave_packet(Dual::var(x)).re)).collect();\nlet df_pts: Vec<(f64, f64)> = xs.iter().map(|&x| (x, shared::wave_packet(Dual::var(x)).eps)).collect();\n\nlet mut svg = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg, (760, 420)).into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(0x14, 0x14, 0x1e)).unwrap();\n\n    let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(&root)\n        .caption(\n            \"f(x) = sin(x)·e^(-x^2/2) and its derivative\",\n            (\"sans-serif\", 18).into_font().color(&WHITE),\n        )\n        .margin(14)\n        .x_label_area_size(34)\n        .y_label_area_size(44)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(-6.0f64..6.0f64, -1.15f64..1.15f64)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart\n        .configure_mesh()\n        .axis_style(RGBColor(0x88, 0x88, 0x99))\n        .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 12).into_font().color(&RGBColor(0xcc, 0xcc, 0xdd)))\n        .light_line_style(RGBColor(0x2a, 0x2a, 0x3a))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart\n        .draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n            f_pts.iter().copied(),\n            RGBColor(0x4f, 0xc3, 0xf7).stroke_width(3),\n        ))\n        .unwrap()\n        .label(\"f(x)\")\n        .legend(|(x, y)| {\n            PathElement::new(vec![(x, y), (x + 20, y)], RGBColor(0x4f, 0xc3, 0xf7).stroke_width(3))\n        });\n\n    chart\n        .draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n            df_pts.iter().copied(),\n            RGBColor(0xe9, 0x45, 0x60).stroke_width(3),\n        ))\n        .unwrap()\n        .label(\"f'(x)  (dual numbers)\")\n        .legend(|(x, y)| {\n            PathElement::new(vec![(x, y), (x + 20, y)], RGBColor(0xe9, 0x45, 0x60).stroke_width(3))\n        });\n\n    chart\n        .configure_series_labels()\n        .position(SeriesLabelPosition::UpperRight)\n        .background_style(RGBColor(0x1e, 0x1e, 0x2a))\n        .border_style(RGBColor(0x55, 0x55, 0x66))\n        .label_font((\"sans-serif\", 13).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_finite_md",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "Dual numbers vs finite differences",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## How exact is \"exact\"? Dual numbers vs finite differences\n\nBefore dual numbers, the default way to get a derivative you did not want to work out by hand was the **finite difference**: nudge the input by a small $h$ and measure the slope. The *central* difference is the good version,\n\n$$f'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h}$$\n\nand it looks unbeatable until you actually chase the accuracy down. It is caught between two errors pulling in opposite directions. **Truncation error** comes from the formula being an approximation; a Taylor expansion pins it at $\\frac{h^2}{6}f'''(\\xi)$, so it shrinks like $O(h^2)$ as you make $h$ smaller. **Roundoff error** comes from computing $f(x+h) - f(x-h)$ in floating point: you are subtracting two nearly equal numbers, which is catastrophic cancellation, then dividing by a tiny $2h$, which inflates the surviving machine noise to $O(\\varepsilon_{\\text{mach}}/h)$ and *grows* as $h$ shrinks.\n\nAdd the two and you get a valley. Minimizing $C_1 h^2 + C_2\\,\\varepsilon_{\\text{mach}}/h$ gives an optimal step and a hard floor:\n\n$$h_\\star \\propto \\varepsilon_{\\text{mach}}^{1/3} \\approx 6\\times 10^{-6}, \\qquad \\text{best error} \\propto \\varepsilon_{\\text{mach}}^{2/3} \\approx 4\\times 10^{-11}$$\n\nSo the best a central difference can do, *tuned perfectly*, is an absolute error around $10^{-11}$. The dual number carries no step size at all, so it has no truncation error and nothing to cancel: it should land near machine precision, roughly $10^{-16}$. The next cell puts all three side by side and lets the numbers make the argument."
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_accuracy",
      "order": 5,
      "label": "Accuracy table + error valley",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "let x0 = 1.0_f64;\nlet f = |x: f64| x.sin() * (-0.5 * x * x).exp();\n\n// Ground truth: the hand-derived derivative.\nlet analytic = (-0.5 * x0 * x0).exp() * (x0.cos() - x0 * x0.sin());\n\n// Forward-mode dual number: exact to machine precision, no step size.\nlet dual = shared::wave_packet(shared::Dual::var(x0)).eps;\n\n// Sweep the step size h = 10^(-k) for the central difference and record the\n// error. The curve (k, log10 error) traces the classic accuracy valley.\nlet mut rows: Vec<Vec<String>> = Vec::new();\nlet mut curve: Vec<(f64, f64)> = Vec::new();\nfor k in 1..=12 {\n    let h = 10f64.powi(-k);\n    let est = (f(x0 + h) - f(x0 - h)) / (2.0 * h);\n    let err = (est - analytic).abs();\n    rows.push(vec![format!(\"1e-{k}\"), format!(\"{est:.12}\"), format!(\"{err:.2e}\")]);\n    curve.push((k as f64, err.max(1e-300).log10()));\n}\nrows.push(vec![\n    \"exact (dual)\".to_string(),\n    format!(\"{dual:.12}\"),\n    format!(\"{:.2e}\", (dual - analytic).abs()),\n]);\n\nlet table = Table::new(\n    vec![\"step h\", \"central difference f'(1)\", \"abs error\"],\n    rows,\n);\n\nlet chart = Plot::line(&curve)\n    .title(\"Central-difference error vs step size\")\n    .x_label(\"k   (step h = 10^-k)\")\n    .y_label(\"log10 |error|\")\n    .size(760, 380)\n    .point_labels(true);\n\n(table, chart)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_act2",
      "order": 6,
      "label": "Reverse mode: the tape",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 2: reverse mode, and why your GPU only does it one way\n\nForward mode has a scaling problem hiding in plain sight. Each pass seeds **one** input's $\\varepsilon$ to 1 and reads back **one** input's partial derivative. A function with $n$ inputs needs $n$ passes to get the full gradient. For our little $g(x,y,z)$ that is 3 passes, no big deal. For a neural network with $10^8$ parameters, that is $10^8$ forward passes per gradient, and training would wrap up sometime after the heat death of the sun.\n\n**Reverse mode flips the cost.** It runs one forward pass to compute the value, recording every operation onto a **tape** (a [Wengert list](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automatic_differentiation#Reverse_accumulation)), then one backward pass that walks the tape in reverse, seeds the output's sensitivity to 1, and accumulates an *adjoint* $\\bar{v} = \\partial(\\text{output})/\\partial v$ into every intermediate. One forward plus one backward yields **the entire gradient**, no matter how many inputs there are. That single asymmetry, cheap gradients of one scalar with respect to millions of parameters, is the whole reason backpropagation exists and the reason every deep-learning framework is reverse-mode under the hood.\n\nThe mechanics are humble. Each node remembers its parents and the local partial $\\partial(\\text{this})/\\partial(\\text{parent})$; the backward sweep multiplies adjoints along the edges and sums where paths merge, which is just the multivariate chain rule in a `for` loop. The shared **`Tape`** and **`Var`** types do exactly this in about sixty lines. The next cell differentiates\n\n$$g(x, y, z) = x\\,y + \\sin(x\\,z) + y^2$$\n\nand pulls all three partials out of a **single** backward pass, cross-checking them against forward mode and against the gradient worked out by hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_reverse",
      "order": 7,
      "label": "One backward pass, every partial",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use crate::shared::{Dual, Tape};\n\n// A scalar function of three inputs, the shape of an ML loss or a physics\n// energy. We want every partial derivative.\n//   g(x, y, z) = x·y + sin(x·z) + y²\nlet (xv, yv, zv) = (0.5_f64, 1.0_f64, 2.0_f64);\n\n// Reverse mode: build the tape, then ONE backward pass fills the whole gradient.\nlet tape = Tape::new();\nlet x = tape.var(xv);\nlet y = tape.var(yv);\nlet z = tape.var(zv);\nlet g = x * y + (x * z).sin() + y * y;\nlet grad = g.grad();\nlet (rx, ry, rz) = (grad[x.id()], grad[y.id()], grad[z.id()]);\n\n// Forward mode for contrast: one evaluation per input (three passes here).\nlet fwd = |sx: f64, sy: f64, sz: f64| {\n    let x = Dual { re: xv, eps: sx };\n    let y = Dual { re: yv, eps: sy };\n    let z = Dual { re: zv, eps: sz };\n    (x * y + (x * z).sin() + y * y).eps\n};\nlet (fx, fy, fz) = (fwd(1.0, 0.0, 0.0), fwd(0.0, 1.0, 0.0), fwd(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n\n// Hand-derived partials, to check against.\nlet ax = yv + zv * (xv * zv).cos();\nlet ay = xv + 2.0 * yv;\nlet az = xv * (xv * zv).cos();\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\n        \"partial\".to_string(),\n        \"reverse mode (1 pass)\".to_string(),\n        \"forward mode (3 passes)\".to_string(),\n        \"hand-derived\".to_string(),\n    ],\n    vec![\n        vec![\"∂g/∂x\".to_string(), format!(\"{rx:.10}\"), format!(\"{fx:.10}\"), format!(\"{ax:.10}\")],\n        vec![\"∂g/∂y\".to_string(), format!(\"{ry:.10}\"), format!(\"{fy:.10}\"), format!(\"{ay:.10}\")],\n        vec![\"∂g/∂z\".to_string(), format!(\"{rz:.10}\"), format!(\"{fz:.10}\"), format!(\"{az:.10}\")],\n    ],\n)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_act3",
      "order": 8,
      "label": "What std::autodiff actually does",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 3: what `std::autodiff` actually does\n\nEverything above we wrote by hand. The compiler feature runs the same two algorithms, except **Enzyme differentiates the LLVM IR after the optimizer has already run**, and that ordering is the entire point. Our `Dual` type, and every source-level autodiff library, differentiates the *source* and then hopes the optimizer cleans up the result. Enzyme differentiates code that has already been inlined, constant-folded, and vectorized, so the derivative *composes* with the optimizations instead of fighting them. That is how it stays fast enough to be worth it.\n\nYou opt in per function with an attribute that names the generated derivative and gives an **activity type** to each argument and return value:\n\n```rust\n#![feature(autodiff)]\nuse std::autodiff::autodiff_reverse;\n\n// Enzyme synthesizes `d_square` from `square`, at the LLVM-IR level.\n#[autodiff_reverse(d_square, Active, Active)]\nfn square(x: f64) -> f64 { x * x }\n// The reverse pass gives df/dx; at x = 3 that is 6.0.\n```\n\nThe activity types are the vocabulary for telling Enzyme what to do with each slot:\n\n- **`Dual`** carries a forward-mode derivative alongside the value, exactly our $\\varepsilon$ slot.\n- **`Active`** marks a scalar that is differentiated in reverse mode and returned by value.\n- **`Duplicated`** pairs an input with a shadow buffer that receives its gradient, the reverse-mode story for arrays and structs.\n- **`Const`** says do not differentiate this one.\n\n**Why can't this run in the cell above?** Look at line one. `#![feature(autodiff)]` is a *crate-root* attribute, and ironpad injects your code into the body of a function inside a crate it controls, so there is nowhere to hang it. The feature also currently wants fat LTO and a `libEnzyme` artifact present at build time, and a browser sandbox compiling to `wasm32` has none of that. This is genuinely a *compiler* feature and not a library you can import, which is exactly why hand-rolling the algorithms is the honest way to feel how they work.\n\nThe roadmap is where it gets greedy. The same effort is landing [`std::offload`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-project-goals/issues/109) (mark a function to run on the GPU through LLVM's offload backend) and a batching attribute (fuse $N$ scalar calls into one vectorized call). Compose `#[offload]` with `#[autodiff]` and you get a **differentiated function running on a GPU**, which is to say Rust-native machine learning and scientific computing with the gradients handled by the compiler instead of a Python framework."
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_outro",
      "order": 9,
      "label": "Status, drivers, and links",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Where it stands, and who to thank\n\nThe honest status, from the [Rust Project Goals update of April 2026](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2026/05/18/project-goals-2026-04/) (published 2026-05-18):\n\n- **Partially in CI.** Autodiff is enabled on some Linux and MinGW runners, not yet everywhere.\n- **Usable today, if you bring your own Enzyme.** You can download a `libEnzyme` artifact, drop it into your nightly toolchain, and build with `-Zautodiff=Enable` and `lto = \"fat\"`. It works; it is just not yet the one-command experience.\n- **macOS is the blocker.** A turnkey rollout is waiting on an LLVM static-to-dynamic linking change on macOS. Once that lands, a `rustup` component will auto-download Enzyme and the whole thing collapses to \"just works on nightly.\"\n- **Rough edges remain:** no `dyn Trait` arguments, fat LTO required, debug builds are flakier, and a few `impl`-block miscompiles are still open.\n\nNearly all of this is the work of **Manuel Drehwald** ([@ZuseZ4](https://github.com/ZuseZ4)), roughly five days a week, sponsored by **LLNL** and the **University of Toronto**, after about three years spent building the LLVM autodiff tooling and a compiler fork before any of it went upstream. If you want the full tour from the source, his RustWeek 2026 talk, [\"std::autodiff: computing derivatives with your compiler\"](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yGHbQTw7e8c), is the one to watch.\n\n**The receipts**, if you want to follow along or contribute:\n\n- [The `std::autodiff` nightly docs](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/autodiff/index.html)\n- [The `-Zautodiff` unstable-book entry](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/unstable-book/compiler-flags/autodiff.html)\n- [Compiler-team integration issue #611](https://github.com/rust-lang/compiler-team/issues/611) and [feature tracking issue #124509](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/124509)\n- [The `std::offload` project goal #109](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-project-goals/issues/109)\n\n*You just built, by hand, the two algorithms a compiler is learning to generate for you. When it ships, you will know exactly what it is doing under the hood, because you already did it once yourself.*"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Hand-roll the two algorithms Rust is baking into the compiler, forward-mode dual numbers and a reverse-mode tape, then meet the real std::autodiff.",
